---
name: code-review
description: Single-pass, read-only code review of the current branch's committed diff for correctness — logic bugs, regressions, edge cases, error handling, data integrity. Runs the shared review engine (review.mjs), returns structured findings by severity, fixes every HIGH and MEDIUM without asking, defers what the one fix check leaves, and reports once. The script never fixes; no loop.
---

# code-review — review → fix → one report

Review the committed diff vs the remote's default branch (auto-detected from `origin/HEAD`) for **code correctness only** (not style, not product scope, not security: it runs with `--code-only`, so it never starts a security pass, whatever the review config says).
One pass, read-only reviewer, findings come back to the seat, every HIGH and MEDIUM is fixed in-context without asking (rule 10 below), then one report.

## Step 1 — run the shared engine

Commit the work first, including the build folder (it reviews the committed diff). Then run the same `review.mjs` every tool shares — repo copy first, shared runtime as the fallback, and also when the repo copy is too old to support `--code-only` (one line says so):
```bash
SCRIPT=scripts/review.mjs
[ -f "$SCRIPT" ] || SCRIPT="$HOME/.agent-config-studio/review-gate/review.mjs"
[ "$SCRIPT" = scripts/review.mjs ] && ! { grep -q -- --supports "$SCRIPT" && node "$SCRIPT" --supports --code-only; } && echo "scripts/review.mjs does not support --code-only; using the shared runtime" >&2 && SCRIPT="$HOME/.agent-config-studio/review-gate/review.mjs"
node "$SCRIPT" --code-only "<intent: what the branch delivers, its acceptance criteria, and anything deliberately out of scope>"
```
Context comes from files: the orchestrator's plan via --plan <plan.md> or REVIEW_PLAN, the builder's adjacent log.md if present, and already-decided findings from the review log. Inside a build loop, pass the plan; a missing plan file refuses. The optional intent string supplements those files. Decided stays decided: same-branch, same-repository rejections arrive as "already decided; don't re-raise without new evidence".
It writes `reviews/<ts>/code-review.md` and prints findings as JSON after a `===FINDINGS_JSON===` marker. Parse that block; keep reviewId and each finding's id, including disputed and carried unlocated findings, for the status file. All retain their stored derived severity; every non-filed finding needs a status.

**Who reviews.** The `reviewer` role in ACS (Models → Roles; `model-id --role reviewer --json`) is the pool, each family at its own flavor effort. A family's CLI follows its model id (`claude-…` → claude, `gpt-…` → codex, `grok-…` → grok); families whose CLI is not installed are skipped and one line names them. The run stops with a message naming the fix — never trying another vendor — when `model-id` is missing (`acs install-model-id`), the role is unset (you choose it at install: `acs packs install review-gate --set-role reviewer=<family>[,…]`) or missing, no family in it has its CLI, or a named reviewer has no CLI.

**Routing by size.** With config `routing` set, e.g. `[{"maxLines": 150, "family": "<small-diff family>"}, {"family": "<everything else>"}]`, the first entry whose `maxLines` the diff's changed lines (added + removed) are under picks the reviewer, and an entry without `maxLines` takes the rest; a diff touching riskPaths is never small. Order: `REVIEW_REVIEWER` / `reviewer`, then a `force` rule, then a pick (`REVIEW_PICK` for one run, or config `pick`, which prints a note that it overrides routing), then routing. Routing ignores crossFamily (a route that lands on the author's vendor says so in the why line), each family runs at its own effort, a family must be in the reviewer role, and fix checks route on the fix diff. A family no entry names (grok, say) reviews only when named. `--show-config` shows the route and why. With no `routing`, the pick decides as before.

**Scoring reviewers (`--eval`).** `node "$SCRIPT" --eval [--family <f>,…] [--cases <dir>] [--repeat N] [--timeout 15m] [--yes]` has each family (default: the reviewer role) review every case in a throwaway repo, in eval mode: no security pass, verify or second opinion, and nothing reaches the review log, a close or the push gate. A case is a folder with `case.json` (`id`, `kind` bug|clean, `intent`; a bug lists `expect: [{file, side, lines, what}]`, a clean case may list `allow`) and `base/` + `head/` trees, kept in `~/.agent-config-studio/review-gate/eval/cases/` (two examples sit in `eval/examples/` beside the script). It prints bugs caught and missed, false alarms on clean cases, and errored reviews per family, and appends one record per family to `review-log/eval-log.jsonl`. Over 10 reviews needs `--yes`.

**Grok runs.** A grok review runs with an empty temporary HOME and the real GROK_HOME, so nothing grok borrows from `~/.claude` or `~/.cursor` (plugin MCP servers, hooks) starts, and its login is the usual one. It is refused while grok would still start an MCP server, run the project's hooks, or run in leader mode (set in any grok config layer or by macOS device management; a layer that can't be read counts as on). `grokMcpAllow` lists servers from grok's own `config.toml` that may start anyway (their tools stay denied): operator-only, in the home config or `REVIEW_GROK_MCP_ALLOW`, never a tracked repo config, and empty by default; ask-model reads the same key from the home config.

**Cross-vendor.** Cross-vendor checks follow the review gate's crossFamily setting (default prefer: another vendor when available, never a blocker; the run names who checked and why). Pass `REVIEW_AUTHOR=<family or vendor that built the branch>` and `crossFamily: "prefer"` (the default) skips the author's vendor when the pool has another; `"off"` never filters. If no other vendor can run, review with what is available — never wait — and say so. The home config is the floor: a tracked repo config, `REVIEW_CONFIG` or `REVIEW_CROSS_FAMILY` may turn it on, never off (`REVIEW_CROSS_FAMILY=off` counts only when no config sets `crossFamily`); `--cross-family` prints the setting in force.
To name one reviewer for a run: `REVIEW_REVIEWER=<family>` (or `codex`, `claude` for the Claude multi-agent team on the role's Claude families, `grok`). Say which reviewer ran.

Per-run knobs (env → config file → built-in): `REVIEW_EFFORT=xhigh` (raise codex reasoning), `REVIEW_BASE=origin/master` (non-standard default branch). The config files are `.claude/review-config.json` (this repo) and `~/.agent-config-studio/review-gate/config.json` (all projects). A repo file sets `verifyCmd`, `classifier`, `base` or a `pick` that names a command only while git doesn't track it; otherwise those are ignored with a warning. Check active config, the merge policy included: `node "$SCRIPT" --show-config`. The review log is `~/.agent-config-studio/review-log/review-log.jsonl` (an existing older log under `~/.claude` keeps being used until that folder exists).

**Docs focus.** `--focus docs` with `REVIEW_PATHS` reviews a slice of comment and doc edits as a full review: the correctness prompt, plus the docs instructions (untrue or unbacked claims, any code-line change, a removed comment that carried a reason, a changed directive). It reviews a slice only. `--since-close <sha>` on a slice gives the reviewer only what changed after a complete close of this branch ended at `<sha>`; it refuses unless that close covered those files there.

**Big diffs: review in slices.** The prompt inlines at most 120k characters of diff; over that, the run warns and names the largest top-level folders.
Slice the one commit instead: run the review once per path group with `REVIEW_PATHS=<pathspec>,<pathspec>` (git pathspecs, comma- or newline-separated; config `paths` also works). A list that matches no changed file is refused.
Each slice gets its own record, findings and close, and a sliced close ignores later changes outside its paths. Commit every fix first, then close every slice at the same HEAD.
**Run the slices in parallel:** `node "$SCRIPT" --slices "<group>;<group>" [--jobs N] --code-only "<intent>"` reviews each group (one `REVIEW_PATHS` value, so commas inside a group, `;` between groups) as its own run, at most N at a time (default 4), in this checkout. Each slice prints under `=== slice i/n: <paths> (exit c) ===` as it finishes; then `===SLICES_JSON===` lists `{paths, exit, reviewId, result}` per slice, `result` being that slice's FINDINGS_JSON. A failed slice makes the run exit non-zero: rerun only that group. A big run outlasts a foreground shell timeout, so start it in the background and wait for it to finish. Then fix, fix-check and close each slice by its review id; one HEAD for all the closes is simplest, though a slice closed earlier still counts.
The slices together must cover every file of the diff: the push gate and `--summary` accept them only then, and a missing slice blocks the push and names the uncovered files. Coverage is by content, so a late commit needs only its own files reviewed as one more slice, closed at the tip; the slices closed earlier still count.

**What the push gate accepts.** A changed file is covered when its exact state at the pushed tip (content and mode, or deleted) was seen by a complete close or a clean fix check (one with no finding that needs a status), logged in this repository at a commit the tip descends from. A review on another branch or in another clone covers nothing. Content already on the default branch needs no coverage: the gate trusts the commit that `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` names (a symbolic ref to an existing `refs/remotes/origin/<branch>`, read locally, never set by the gate, never from `REVIEW_BASE` or config `base`), and only for a push to `origin`; any other remote, or a missing, detached or dangling origin/HEAD, keeps the full rule. A file whose diff the 120k cap cut is not covered, and a file changed on both sides of a merge needs a review that saw the merge. `builds/` files need no coverage. The tip also needs one complete close at it (or followed only by `builds/` commits), so the tests ran there. A refusal lists the uncovered files: review just those with `REVIEW_PATHS` and close that slice at the tip. **Update a reviewed branch by merging the default branch into it, never by rebasing** (a rebase needs coverage again). After a clean merge, re-close the existing review at the merge tip: the tests run there and no model is called. A file changed on both sides of the merge, conflict or not, and any file edited inside the merge commit itself, gets a slice review at the tip (`REVIEW_PATHS` = the files the refusal names) closed there; the re-close then refuses (with a fix check) or goes partial (without one) naming those same files, which is expected, and the slice's close is the one the push needs. Generated dependency lockfiles (package-lock.json, yarn.lock, pnpm-lock.yaml, Cargo.lock, go.sum and the others the gate lists, by exact file name in any folder) are exempt by default; the refusal and `--summary` name every exempt file as not reviewed, so the PR shows them. The operator can list more files in `coverageExempt` (globs, like `riskPaths`) or turn the defaults off with `"coverageExemptDefaults": false`: in the home config (`coverageExempt` also through `REVIEW_COVERAGE_EXEMPT`) or an untracked repo config, never a tracked one. A tracked repo config never drops these home-config values either: the home file's `coverageExempt`, `coverageExemptDefaults`, `grokMcpAllow`, `verifyCmd`, `classifier` and `base` stay in force over it. Agents don't add to the list on their own. A refusal for any other file whose diff alone is over the cap goes in the PR as a blocker for the operator, never as a shell command for them to paste.

## Step 2 — triage, then fix without asking

Verify every finding against the actual code before fixing (you have the diff loaded):
- **Keep the scores and derived severity.** The script derives severity from scores; reviewer labels are comparison text only. Scores cannot change in triage.
- **Derived severity.** Reviewers supply likelihood and impact (integers 1–10), protected (boolean) and a concrete trigger. HIGH is protected or risk ≥ 40; LOW is unprotected risk ≤ 10 and is filed; MEDIUM is the rest. Fix HIGH first, then MEDIUM. HIGH, MEDIUM and all unscored findings block until fixed, REFUTED or waived. Invalid scores are re-asked once; still-unscored findings block as MEDIUM (HIGH if protected). Legacy unscored LOW findings also block. Filed findings need no close status, are already decided, and are fixed only when the operator picks them. Grade criterion FAILs stay HIGH and are never filed.
- **Protected cases.** A demonstrated reachable exploit, stored data lost beyond ordinary undo, wrong money, or a way around the review gate in the gate's own logic: the push check, the close, a dispute or a waiver accepting work it should refuse.
  Guard command phrasings (agent-guard) are not protected; score them by likelihood × impact because the guard is an accident barrier.
- **Reject with a reason and evidence** (file:line or a test). HIGH/MEDIUM and unscored rejections need a REFUTED verdict from --dispute, a prior REFUTED reference on the same branch and repository, or an operator waiver. One real verdict per finding; no back-and-forth. NO-VERDICT means it stands and may be retried. If CONFIRMED, fix it. When the reviewer role has only one family, so no second family can give a verdict, only an operator waiver can reject the finding: --waive from a real terminal is the operator's own tool, and agents never ask for it (rule 9).
- **Pre-existing findings.** A reviewer marks `preExisting: true` when the defect exists at the base and this branch neither introduced it nor made it newly reachable; the script then records `inDiff`: whether any of its places is within 3 lines of a line the branch changed (the merge-base review diff). `preExisting: true` with `inDiff: false` is pre-existing: it never blocks, needs no status, is never deferred, and `--summary` lists it with its score under "Pre-existing (not introduced here)". A protected one is listed first as "Pre-existing HIGH" and makes the merge tier HIGH (no agent merge past a known exploit) without blocking the close. With `inDiff` true or null the claim is ignored and the finding blocks as usual. Original review findings only; a fix-check finding is never pre-existing.
- **Grouped findings.** One defect in several places comes back once, with its other places in `instances` (file, line and quote each). Each instance is quote-checked; an unlocated one is dropped with a note. Fix every place: one status covers them all, and a fixed grouped finding authorizes every instance's file at close.

**No confirmation (rule 10).** Send every HIGH and MEDIUM straight to the fix round, or dispute it with proof; never ask which to fix. A finding that is really a product choice (bug or feature?) gets the safe fix by default and is named in the report so the operator can reverse it. LOWs are filed. **One report, after the fix round and the fix check:** what was fixed, what was deferred and what was filed, each with its numbers (rule 11), plus any product-choice fix the operator may reverse; fixing the filed items is optional. Clean reviews still need --close and tests; a clean report alone is not done.

## Step 3 — apply the fixes in-context

Fix the real cause directly (you have the diff loaded; do NOT re-spawn a model to fix). Keep fixes minimal and faithful; never suppress or paper over. Then run the repo's linter/formatter on the changed files and commit before --close, never afterward. Fixes never get a full re-review. Every non-filed finding needs a fixed or rejected status; unknown keys and any severity field refuse:

```json
{"f1":{"status":"fixed"},"f2":{"status":"rejected","reason":"<why>","evidence":"<file:line or test>","refutedBy":{"review":"<prior review id>","finding":"<prior finding id>"}}}
```

After the fix check, its x… findings (and a Grade recheck's r… findings, or a criterion still failing its recheck) may instead be deferred: `{"x1":{"status":"deferred","reason":"<why>","fix":"<fix idea>"}}`. The reason is required; any severity or score field refuses; an original finding can't be deferred.

Label what each fixed or rejected finding turned out to be with `response`: on `fixed`, `"real"` or `"unimportant"` (fixed though not worth it; a fix without a label is logged as real); on `rejected`, `"wrong"` (not a defect) or `"intentional"` (the behavior is meant), required on every rejection, e.g. `{"f2":{"status":"rejected","reason":"<why>","evidence":"<file:line>","response":"intentional"}}`. A label never replaces the REFUTED verdict or waiver a blocking rejection needs. `node "$SCRIPT" --noise-report [--since <date>] [--all]` counts, per reviewer family and severity band, real / unimportant / wrong / intentional / unlabeled / deferred / filed / pre-existing findings from each review's latest close (this repository unless `--all`); legacy statuses count as unlabeled.

Omit refutedBy for this review's own REFUTED verdict or a waiver. The orchestrator disputes serious rejections and closes against the committed fixes:

```bash
node "$SCRIPT" --dispute <review-id> <finding-id> --reason "<why>" --evidence "<file:line or test>"
node "$SCRIPT" --close <review-id> --status builds/<slug>/status.json
node "$SCRIPT" --summary
```

Close runs the whole test suite using verifyCmd from .claude/review-config.json or --test-cmd. If there are no tests, pass --no-tests "<reason>"; it is logged and appears in the summary. Only when close requires it, the orchestrator runs --fix-check <review-id> once. Fix, dispute or defer its x1… findings, close, then a hard stop, with no further model pass. Config `fixCheck`: `"risk"` (default: a fix touching tests, verify scripts or riskPaths needs a fix check) | `"always"` (the risk rule, plus any other code fix after the review; other docs, static, coverage-exempt and `builds/` files never need one, so it is never looser than "risk"; for a Grade, its recheck counts as the check when nothing either rule counts changed after it). The home config is the floor: a repo file, `REVIEW_CONFIG` or `REVIEW_FIX_CHECK` may raise it, never lower it. Fixes land before the fix check: commits after it may touch only `builds/` and test files, and close still runs the full tests. A file the fix check saw is in scope at close, so a fix to a sibling file is fine once the fix check has seen it. Close counts a changed file as fixed only when it equals, exactly, the path the script recorded when it checked that finding's quote; a finding with no recorded path (unlocated, ambiguous between a/ or b/ and the bare path, a Grade finding, or one logged before paths were recorded) covers no file. A rename counts as a delete plus an add, so the renamed path is never covered by a finding. A fixed finding covers its whole file, not only the lines it cited. A complete close covers what it saw at HEAD (see what the push gate accepts, above); a partial close lists outOfScope files and covers nothing. Use refutedBy for an already-decided finding rather than reopening its dispute without new evidence. Put --summary output in the PR body. --log-outcome is optional telemetry only; it never closes a review.

## Review policy (rules 1–11)

1. The operator is involved only at PR merge; no agent pauses work to ask the operator to run a command.
2. First round: every HIGH and MEDIUM from the original review is fixed, or rejected with a REFUTED verdict from a different model (`--dispute`). Nothing is deferred in this round; LOWs are filed automatically.
3. One fix round, then one fix check. What the fix check finds (`x…`, any severity) may be deferred: `{"x1":{"status":"deferred","reason":"<why>","fix":"<fix idea>"}}`. The reason is required, `fix` is optional (the reviewer's suggestion shows otherwise), and the reviewer's score stands. The close then completes; there is no second round. One exception, in this flow rather than in the gate: when the fix check raises a HIGH in the fix itself (`inFix: true`), defer it at close (the close completes), fix it in one more commit, then review that commit's files as one slice (`REVIEW_PATHS=<those files>`) and close the slice at the tip; whatever that slice's own fix check finds is deferred. Hard stop: no third pass.
4. Grade: a criterion still failing after one fix round and its one recheck may be deferred as "criterion not met" with the same status shape, and so may the recheck's own `r…` findings. It never silently passes.
5. The PR body (`--summary`) lists, in order: "Acceptance criteria not met", deferred HIGHs, then all other deferred findings; `--deferred` lists them too. Agents merge only through merge.mjs, which follows the review gate's merge policy (default off: the operator merges). Never gh pr merge, auto-merge or the merge button. The merge is the approval, and the operator can send the branch back.
   **Merge tier** (`--summary` only reports it; merge.mjs below acts on it). Each close logs a tier, and `--summary` prints `Merge tier: LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH (<reasons>). Policy <policy>: would merge | needs a human`. HIGH: a riskPaths or `merge.alwaysHuman` file (plus `.github/workflows/**` and `package.json`), a deferred HIGH or protected finding, a protected pre-existing finding, a security pass the config needs that no review ran, a partial close or `--no-tests`; LOW: nothing deferred and only docs, tests or static files changed; MEDIUM otherwise.
   `merge.policy` is `off` (default: every tier needs a human) | `strict` (LOW, docs and tests only) | `standard` (LOW) | `always` (LOW and MEDIUM; never HIGH). `merge.classifier` (default null) may move only a MEDIUM to LOW at confidence ≥ `merge.threshold` (0.8); on any failure the rules' tier stands, and the record logs `tierBy` and its probabilities.
   A tracked repo config may only make `merge.policy` stricter and cannot set `merge.classifier`; set them in the home config or an untracked repo config.
   **Agent merge.** After opening the PR, the orchestrator runs `node ~/.agent-config-studio/review-gate/merge.mjs` once, always the shared runtime's copy (it uses the repo's `scripts/review.mjs` when there is one; `--pr <n>` names the PR, `--dry-run` only reports). Agents never merge any other way: no `gh pr merge`, no auto-merge, no merge button. It reads `--tier-json` and does one thing: policy `off` does nothing and calls no gh (`none`, exit 0); a tier the policy merges is merged with a merge commit at HEAD (`merged`, exit 0; a base with a merge queue needs a human and is never merged; a merge that only queued the PR or set auto-merge is taken back (dequeued, or auto-merge disabled) and needs a human) only when the PR is open, not a draft, its head is local HEAD, its base is the review's base, the tree is clean, GitHub reports no conflict, branch-protection block or requested changes, no `needs-human` label is on it, the base has not moved (`BEHIND`), and every check passes or is skipped; anything else needs a human: `merge.onHuman` `comment` (default) adds the `needs-human` label and one comment (`merge.humanComment`, then the tier line and why), and a PR that already has the label gets no new comment (`labeled`, exit 10); `none` writes nothing (exit 11). A `BLOCKED` PR (branch protection) needs a human. An `UNKNOWN` merge state is read again up to 3 times, 5 seconds apart. An unsafe state (head, base, draft, closed, dirty tree, conflict, still `UNKNOWN`, gh failure, failed merge) is `refused`, exit 2, with no write. Zero CI checks need a human unless `merge.requireChecks` is false; a failing or pending check never merges. `merge.allowBehind: true` lets a `BEHIND` PR merge. A tracked repo config, or a file `REVIEW_CONFIG` names (unless it is the home config), may set `merge.onHuman`, but never `merge.humanComment` or `merge.classifier`, may only make `merge.policy` stricter, and may only set `merge.requireChecks` to true and `merge.allowBehind` to false. Each run logs an intent and an outcome record to merge-log.jsonl beside the review log. Report the one line it prints; never merge a PR it did not merge.
6. Agents never re-rate a finding (severity and score fields stay refused), defer in the first round, edit, re-log or forge review records, or ask the operator to run `--waive` or `REVIEW_GATE_OVERRIDE`.
7. A history rewrite (squash, split, rebase) after a review or Grade is allowed when the final tree is byte-identical: the gate compares tree hashes with the review, Grade, fix-check, recheck and close heads, and logs the carry-over as `carried`. Any other tree gets a fresh review.
8. An unexpected gate refusal: one fresh review of the final branch through the normal path, then a clean close. A refusal after merging the default branch is not unexpected: re-close the existing review at the merge tip, and slice-review only the both-sides files it names; never a fresh review of the whole branch. If the gate still refuses, stop and tell the operator in chat, quoting the gate's message.
9. `--waive` and `REVIEW_GATE_OVERRIDE` remain the operator's own emergency tools from the operator's terminal; no normal flow uses them.
10. No confirmation before fixing: every HIGH and MEDIUM goes straight to the fix round, or is disputed with proof, without asking. A finding that is really a product choice gets the safe fix by default and is named in the report so the operator can reverse it. The operator hears once, after the fix round and fix check: what was fixed and what was filed. When the rule 3 slice ran, that report comes after the slice's close. Fixing the filed items is optional.
11. Show the numbers: every finding shown to the operator carries its scores, e.g. `LOW 6/100 (likelihood 2/10 × impact 3/10)`, or `unscored`, never just the word. The report, `--summary` and `--deferred` print them that way.

## Hard rules

- Read-only reviewer; it never edits or commits.
- Never weaken a check to make a finding disappear.
- Cross-vendor checks follow crossFamily (see Step 1); never wait for a reviewer.
- Builders commit at phase end and never run reviews, grades or other models themselves. The orchestrator handles those checks, pushes and opens the PR with the review summary.
- Never push to a protected branch. Agents merge only through merge.mjs, which follows the review gate's merge policy (default off: the operator merges). Never gh pr merge, auto-merge or the merge button.

Every PR body must include a section titled exactly **Deferred findings**, using the deferred and filed findings in --summary (or `none` when empty). When chasing a bug, first check `node <review.mjs> --deferred` and merged PR descriptions with `gh pr list --state merged --search "Deferred findings in:body"`. Resolve <review.mjs> repo-first, with the shared runtime fallback above. `--deferred` lists this repo by repoId, newest first; add `--all` for every repo. The trigger is the input or call path named in the issue.

Use `node <review.mjs> --rubric` for the current version and score anchors. `~/.agent-config-studio/review-gate/calibration.md` contains the eight reference cases; run `node <review.mjs> --calibrate` (or `--calibrate copy.md`) after rubric changes or when evaluating a new model. Historical log records retain their stored bands and filing decisions.

