packs/review-gate/skills/claude/code-review/SKILL.md:26:- **Who reviews.** Reviewers come from the `reviewer` role in ACS (Models → Roles; `model-id --role reviewer --json`) — an unranked pool. Each family runs at its own flavor effort (`model-id --flavor <family> --json`), and its CLI follows its model id: `claude-…` → claude, `gpt-…` → codex, `grok-…` → grok. Families whose CLI is not installed are skipped, and one line names them. With no reviewer named, hard rules run first — `crossFamily`, then any config `force` rule (e.g. `{ "paths": ["**/auth/**", "**/payments/**"], "family": "<family>" }`, or `"paths": "risk"` for the `riskPaths` list) — then `pick` chooses: `first` (default, pool order), `rotate` (the family with the fewest logged reviews), a family name, or the command of an optional local classifier (it sees the diff's paths and line counts plus each family's flavor likes/quirks — never code; below confidence 0.7, or if it fails, the first family is used; its probabilities are printed and logged). `crossFamily: "prefer"` (default) skips pool members from the author's vendor when another remains — **pass `REVIEW_AUTHOR=<family or vendor that built the branch>`** when you know it; unknown, nothing is filtered and the run says so. Cross-vendor checks follow the review gate's crossFamily setting (default prefer: another vendor when available, never a blocker; the run names who checked and why). `"off"` never filters. The home config is the floor: a tracked repo config, `REVIEW_CONFIG` or `REVIEW_CROSS_FAMILY` may turn it on, never off (`REVIEW_CROSS_FAMILY=off` counts only when no config sets `crossFamily`); `--cross-family` prints the setting in force. The run prints one line naming the reviewer and why.
packs/review-gate/skills/claude/code-review/SKILL.md:129:Resolve the judge family with `eval "$(model-id --role judge || echo 'echo "model-id failed: run acs install-model-id (see the Agent Config Studio README)" >&2; exit 1')"` (it sets `family`, `id` and `effort`).
packs/review-gate/skills/codex/context-pass/SKILL.md:36:Resolve the writer: `eval "$(model-id --role writer || echo 'echo "model-id failed: run acs install-model-id (see the Agent Config Studio README)" >&2; exit 1')"` sets `family`, `id` and `effort`. An unset role stops here with its fix (ACS → Models → Roles, or `acs packs install review-gate --set-role writer=<family>[,…]`); no model is picked for you. This harness runs a Codex writer: use the role's first family whose id starts with `gpt-` (the families, in order, are in `model-id --role writer --json`), at its effort. When the role has none, stop and say so. Never run the writer through ask-model, which is read-only.
packs/review-gate/skills/claude/context-pass/SKILL.md:40:Resolve the writer: `eval "$(model-id --role writer || echo 'echo "model-id failed: run acs install-model-id (see the Agent Config Studio README)" >&2; exit 1')"` sets `family`, `id` and `effort`. An unset role stops here with its fix: choose it in ACS → Models → Roles, or `acs packs install review-gate --set-role writer=<family>[,…]`. No model is picked for you.
packs/review-gate/skills/claude/context-pass/SKILL.md:42:Run the writer as a **native subagent**, so the operator can watch it: the Agent tool, with `model` set to the role's first family whose id starts with `claude-` (the role's families, in order, are in `model-id --role writer --json`). When the role has no Claude family, stop and say so: this harness runs a Claude writer. Never run the writer through ask-model, which is read-only.
